\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Turkey Examines Lira Trades\b0\par\par\uc2 840 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2013\par 00:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par C3\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (FROM THE WALL STREET JOURNAL 7/11/13) \par    By Emre Peker and Kerim Karakaya \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ISTANBUL -- Turkey started an investigation of foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  transactions, building on a probe of stock trading, as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  broadened a fight to shore up the lira, moves analysts say threaten to undermine confidence in the country.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Banking Regulation and Supervision Agency has asked Turkey's major banks for detailed information on clients who sold the lira on Monday and Tuesday, as well as the sums of money and profit margins involved in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trades, three bankers said. The BRSA said in a statement that the probe is "routine." The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 The probes come as \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is selling record amounts of dollars in daily auctions to counter a slide that has weakened the lira by about 9% in the past two months, a move that makes it one of the worst-performing emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Analysts say both the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the investigations threaten investors' confidence in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to weather a global selloff in developing economices.\par \par \uc2 The effort to stabilize markets follows antigovernment protests that roiled Turkey in June, just as international investors were pulling out of emerging markets because of concern that the U.S. Federal Reserve will end policies that had encouraged investment there.\par \par \uc2 "Turkey is always a higher risk country than other emerging markets when we talk about portfolio outflows and in a sudden stop, Turkey is always the most vulnerable one," said Valentina Chen, who helps manage $4.5 billion of emerging-market debt and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Aviva Investors in London. "With these investigations, I think they're making the situation worse. Turkey is an accident waiting to happen."\par \par \uc2 Since June 11, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began auctioning dollars for lira, Turkey has used about 12% of its net foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. Economist estimate the total has fallen to just above $40 billion. Erdem Basci, governor of the \uc2 Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has declined to raise interest rates, a tactic countries often use when their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are plunging.\par \par \uc2 The country's Achilles' heel is its chronic current-account deficit, which climbed to $51.3 billion -- 6.5% of Turkey's $786 billion gross domestic product -- in April, a total that exceeds the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's net reserves of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In 2011, when the deficit grew to 10% of GDP, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped 20% against the dollar and helped push inflation to double-digit percentages.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the lira sank to an all-time low of 1.9736 against the dollar, prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the greenback, slightly tightening monetary policy by reducing the amount of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold a record $2.25 billion in seven auctions on Monday, and followed up with another $1.3 billion on Wednesday, but the lira barely budged. It was at 1.9609 to the dollar in late afternoon trading.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's firepower. . .is extremely low by emerging-market standards," said Neil Shearing, chief emerging-markets economist with Capital Economics. "In the end, we suspect the market pressure will win out, particularly given the small size of Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves."\par \par \uc2 The government, which has been quick to blame speculators for the rout, seemed to acknowledge this week that broader factors are behind the selloff.\par \par \uc2 "If the Turkish lira weakness is sustained, it would tend to get inflationary pressure up," Finance Minister Mehmet Simsek said Wednesday at a European Policy Centre event in Brussels. "The fallout so far is very limited, but how we move from here is important. The faster Federal Reserve policy normalization is actually. . .problematic for all emerging markets."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Simsek said in a message from his official \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account that the protests and the prospect that the Fed will scale back its easing efforts were the two causes behind rising borrowing costs in Turkey.\par \par \uc2 Still, despite the recognition that broader factors are at play, the government continues to investigate traders' actions.\par \par \uc2 Two weeks ago, the Capital Markets Board, Turkey's securities regulator, launched an investigation into a selloff in stocks in June that caused Turkey's main share index to plummet 24% from a record high on May 22.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Turkey's two-year borrowing costs have been climbing steadily from an all-time low of 4.79% on May 17, when Moody's Investors Service gave the country a second investment-grade credit rating. \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  raised Turkey to investment grade in November.\par \par \uc2 Yields on benchmark two-year debt rose to 9.25% on Wednesday, the highest level in more than a year.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Recep Tayyip Erdogan has blamed what he calls the "interest-rate lobby" for the protests, saying the unrest is intended to allow investors to profit from Turkey's instability. He has repeatedly warned investors who have sold Turkish assets that the authorities will punish them.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 10, 2013 18:57 ET (22:57 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130710e97a0067l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
