\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Extends Rally As Fed Talks Slower Stimulus\b0\par\par\uc2 776 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 2013\par 22:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global News Select\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By William L. Watts and Sara Sjolin, MarketWatch\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar trimmed an earlier surge but remained higher versus most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday, building on gains triggered a day earlier after Federal Reserve Chairman \uc2 Ben Bernanke\uc2  said the bank could begin to slow its stimulus-providing program of bond purchases by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 Emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tumbled sharply, with the dollar surging to an all-time high versus the Turkish lira, as a stampede out of emerging-market assets continued after the Fed remarks. The dollar's overall rise was aided by a weaker-than-expected Chinese purchasing managers' index.\par \par \uc2 "The bottom line for the dollar is that heightened tapering risks reinforce the 'stronger U.S. dollar' theme that we expect to gain traction into 2014 as U.S. cyclical leadership becomes more evident and U.S. [Treasury] yields rise for the 'right' reasons," said Syed Mansoor Mohi-uddin, strategist at \uc2 UBS\uc2 . "Expect broad-based dollar gains in the G-10 space."\par \par \uc2 The ICE dollar index (DXY), which tracks the greenback's performance against six rivals, rose to 81.823 from 81.301 late Wednesday in North America, and traded as high as 82.140.\par \par \uc2 The dollar extended gains after data showed May existing-home sales rose to their strongest pace since Movember 2009 and the \uc2 Philadelphia Fed\uc2  index also beat expectations.\par \par \uc2 The dollar (USDJPY) changed hands at 97.38 yen, up from Yen96.36 in North American trade late Wednesday, when it surged versus the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The dollar traded as high as Yen98.28 in earlier action Thursday. Meanwhile, the euro (EURUSD) fell to $1.3221, down from $1.3294.\par \par \uc2 The British pound (GBPUSD) managed to shake off early weakness to rise to $1.5492, up slightly from $1.5482.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the dollar traded as high as 1.9503 Turkish lira (USDTRY), setting an all-time high, and remained up 1.5% in recent action at 1.9346 lira. The move came even as \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  added to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts aimed at propping up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , selling $350 million into the market, according to Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 Most of Thursday's across-the-board weakness versus the dollar "can be attributed to the spike in global bond yields, which boosted the cost of borrowing around the world. This accelerated deleveraging in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market with dollar strength only adding to the pain," said \uc2 Kathy Lien\uc2 , managing director of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy for BK Asset Management.\par \par \uc2 The WSJ Dollar Index , which uses a slightly wider comparison basket, rose to 73.86 from Wednesday's close at 73.39.\par \par \uc2 Included in the WSJ Dollar Index is the Australian dollar, which was hit hard after the Federal Reserve said if the U.S. economy improves in line with its forecasts, then it will have scope to reduce the pace of monthly bond purchases. The purchases, directed at stimulating growth, are currently set at $85 billion a month.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie (AUDUSD) changed hands at 92 U.S. cents in recent action after trading as low as 91.63 cents versus 93.01 late Wednesday. The Aussie hadn't traded below 93 cents since September 2010, according to FactSet data.\par \par \uc2 "[A]nyone wanting to know where the AUD/USD is headed needs to firmly have a view on the U.S. fixed-income market," IG chief market strategist Chris Weston wrote to clients Thursday.\par \par \uc2 If the yield on the U.S. 10-year note (10_YEAR) is headed for 3% "over time then the [\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] pair will head firmly below [90 U.S. cents], despite every hedge fund and leveraged player being max short already," said Weston.\par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a higher yield tends to appreciate faster than its rivals. Treasury yields soared as prices fell Thursday, pushing the yield on the 10-year note to 2.42%, the highest yield in 2013.\par \par \uc2 While the dollar had pulled back recently on uncertainty about the Fed's plans for bond buying, analysts have said the quantitative-easing purchases overall have weighed on the dollar's value.\par \par \uc2 The Fed said it may end asset purchases around mid-2014, depending on economic data.\par \par \uc2 "The move by the Fed yesterday might in fact be the beginning of the first dollar uptrend in years not driven by financial market stress," analysts at \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  said in a note.\par \par \uc2 "If we add the still downbeat news from, e.g., China and negative news from many emerging markets like Turkey and Brazil, the selloff in high yielders and commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  might very well have more potential," they added. [ 06-20-13 1639ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020130620e96k0005o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
