\par\par \b \uc2 DJ 2nd UPDATE: Turkish Police Move to Clear Istanbul Square\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joe Parkinson And Emre Peker \par \uc2 1,297 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juni 2013\par 13:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL--Turkey's prime minister defended a police push to clear protesters from a central square in Istanbul on Tuesday that had sparked clashes in the heart of the city, following more than two weeks of nationwide demonstrations.\par \par \uc2 Security forces moved into Taksim Square, the Istanbul epicenter of protests, around 7:30 a.m. on Tuesday, firing tear gas and water cannons, while protesters threw rocks and Molotov cocktails. Battalions of police, which had ceded control of Taksim the day after nationwide protests flared on May 31, were positioned around the square's perimeter by 8 a.m.\par \par \uc2 As clashes began to break out and scenes from the square broadcast live on television, Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sank to fresh 2011 lows, prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to swiftly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilize the lira.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Recep Tayyip Erdogan offered a robust defense of the police incursion, reiterating that the protests were illegal and being spurred on by radical groups and shadowy foreign elements.\par \par \uc2 "This is an illegal uprising but some are trying to mask it," Mr. Erdogan said in Ankara. Delegates at the party headquarters chanted "Turkey is proud of you," in a stronger sign of the polarization between party supporters and the demonstrators around Taksim.\par \par \uc2 "These protests were used on purpose and on a systematic basis. There is a huge game they want to play on Turkey," the prime minister said, telling his party base that democracy was thriving in the country in a way unimaginable a decade ago, when his party swept to power.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Erdogan spoke shortly after Istanbul Governor Huseyin Avni Mutlu convened a news conference to draw a distinction between violent protesters at Taksim who the government says are damaging the country's image, and peaceful demonstrators at the adjacent Gezi Park, a patch of grassland that activists are trying to save from a government rebuilding project. Protests there spawned wider demonstrations.\par \par \uc2 The comments, coupled with the police action, could be aimed at isolating the more radical protesters, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "The police \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to dismantle barricades," Mr. Mutlu, the governor, said. "Some individuals have clashed with the police...We are trying to normalize the scene in Taksim. We are not going to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Gezi Park."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's demonstrations, the broadest challenge to Prime Minister Erdogan's party in 10 years, have brought to light a vibrant contingent of secular and middle-class Turks who are chafing at what they see as an increasingly heavy hand in the prime minister's rule.\par \par \uc2 They have also underscored the strong support Mr. Erdogan enjoys in a vast stretch of the country, largely among nationalists, the religiously pious, and a section of the business class that has profited from his rule.\par \par \uc2 Turkish officials on Tuesday emphasized the damage to the country's unity, image, and financial stability that they say the protests at Taksim have caused. Governor Mutlu said authorities "will not allow a siege" around a statue at the square of Mustafa Kemal Atat\u252\'00\'FCrk, founder of Turkey's republic, and a nearby cultural center, vowing not to allow protest material back around the statue.\par \par \uc2 "The Ataturk statue and cultural center have become like an advertisement board. It's very bad for the image of Turkey and Istanbul," the governor said, adding that the authorities are committed "to be very careful and sensitive in handling the situation."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $50 million at an intraday auction after the lira had plummeted in the morning. The move came shortly after the bank said in a statement it would hold intraday \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling auctions, which it hasn't done since January 2012, to raise the value of the lira against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 By early afternoon in Taksim, police forces withdrew from the square's center and clashes calmed down. Demonstrators gathered on the steps of Gezi Park chanting anti-government slogans. Inside the park, protesters who had camped overnight grew agitated, and fearful of the escalation in violence.\par \par \uc2 Some began to question whether demonstrators were egging on the police force with violence. Others in the park chanted, in unison, to the police: "Go ahead and gas us."\par \par \uc2 "The authorities are trying to turn us into the guilty party as we currently stand on the right side of history," said a 40-year-old tourist guide, who said he rushed to the area after seeing broadcasts of security forces amassing to move into the square earlier in the day.\par \par \uc2 "They know there will be resistance in the park, they know there will be people who throw stones at the police. If they thought people wouldn't respond, they wouldn't send thousands of cops to clear the square. How could people keep calm in the park? It's being gassed," he said.\par \par \uc2 Istanbul Governor Mutlu reiterated that the aim of the police incursion Tuesday was to remove banners from around the area. He also said the government was open to dialogue not just with the country's youth, but with other political parties as well, "for the sake of our nation and for peace."\par \par \uc2 "Do you think Gezi Park hasn't been provoked?" he asked, praising those sitting in at the park for not yielding to provocation by the "marginal groups' to join violence.\par \par \uc2 "Again, I want to publicly state that we are thankful for their sensibility," he said, adding he was optimistic the scene would settle.\par \par \uc2 Earlier Tuesday, police moved to clear barricades and disperse crowds on Siraselviler Avenue leading up to Taksim Square. Security forces fired tear gas at protesters as they fled the area, with one vehicle catching fire in the process.\par \par \uc2 An armored police vehicle mounted with a water cannon, which had also been used to spray demonstrators with pepper gas, tore down an unmanned, makeshift protester barricade at the entry of Siraselviler and doused the burning vehicle with water.\par \par \uc2 The police action came a day after Prime Minister Recep Tayyip Erdogan made his first conciliatory gesture toward the protests that are entering a third week over concerns that new government policies will restrict lifestyles.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Erdogan defied protesters with back-to-back rallies drawing tens of thousands of his supporters in three cities over the weekend. He continued to say the protests were being hijacked by extremists, while also conceding that the he is open to hear the democratic demand of environmentalist groups. The prime minister and his deputies have said that they won't allow illegal demonstrations to continue.\par \par \uc2 "We have been waiting for this to happen," said protester Ugur Hacan, a 24-year-old artistic director for TV series and movies, who was sleeping at the park with friends when police entered the square.\par \par \uc2 "As long as the police don't interfere with Gezi Park, the protests will continue. I believe more people might come after this action," Mr. Hacan said. He and three other friends at the park said police didn't confront the protesters directly.\par \par \uc2 Taksim Square subway was still functioning Tuesday, and some commuters emerging from the metro were caught in the melee.\par \par \uc2 "We need to talk rather than fight each other. I hope this can be resolved quickly," said Can Ozdemir, a hotel waiter, as his eyes streamed from the tear gas.\par \par \uc2 Speaking after a weekly cabinet meeting in Ankara Monday, Deputy Prime Minister Bulent Arinc said Mr. Erdogan would meet some demonstration representatives Wednesday and others at another time, without providing additional details.\par \par \uc2 --Yeliz Candemir and Gokce Sanlialp contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Joe Parkinson at Joe.Parkinson@wsj.com and Emre Peker at Emre.Peker@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 11, 2013 07:39 ET (11:39 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130611e96b0024i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
