\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ Blog:Turkey Protests: What to Expect From the Markets?\b0\par\par\uc2 562 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juni 2013\par 18:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's MoneyBeat blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 By Yeliz Candemir and Joe Parkinson\par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL-- After a weekend of protests that have shaken Turkey, pollsters and analysts are trying to decipher what the demonstrations mean for the country's political future.\par \par \uc2 But what about Turkey's economy, which even before the protest movement began on Tuesday, was reeling from one of its worst weeks in the past two years?\par \par \uc2 Turkish assets have plunged in recent days in line with an emerging markets selloff driven by investor concerns of an early end to the U.S. Federal's bond-buying program.Turkish bond prices have risen dramatically signalling declining investor confidence, while stocks are under pressure. Market jitters sent the lira on Friday to its lowest level in one-and-a-half years, prompting \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Erdem Basci to verbally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy has won plaudits in recent years and was recently awarded its second investment-grade credit rating by \uc2 Moody's\uc2 . But it also remains acutely vulnerable to a decline in foreign investor confidence as it carries a large current account deficit equivalent to 6% of GDP.\par \par \uc2 Middle East Real Time interviewed four analysts to see what they thought the nationwide anti-government protests would mean for Turkish assets when the market opens on Monday. Economists were unified that the lira would be under pressure, but divided on how seriously the market would take the weekend's demonstrations given that Turkey's Prime Minister retains broad support across large parts of the country.\par \par \uc2 Tim Ash, head of emerging market research at \uc2 Standard Bank PLC\uc2  in London: "I think most investors know Turkey quite well and understand that Erdogan and the AKP are still the dominant political force in the country...I actually think that given the demonstrations over the weekend, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Turkish authorities will be more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  and eager that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not move much weaker, as they will be fearful that markets could becomevicious."\par \par \uc2 Tufan Comert, a strategist with Istanbul-based brokerage Garanti Securities: "We may see some further sell off in Turkish assets. Bourse Istanbul 100 Index may start trading Monday with a sharp drop. However, after the first reactions, the protests wouldn't have a dramatic impact on the market as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to tighten liquidity policy further."\par \par \uc2 Inan Demir, chief economist with Finansinvest: "The prevailing shaky sentiment towards risk assets implies that Turkish lira denominated assets could be subject to the same risk as well. So the continuation of large scale protests could exacerbate the pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and TRY denominated assets, particularly if this week's loaded data agenda which includes inflation at home and non-farm payrolls in US sustains the negative sentiment."\par \par \uc2 Ugur Gurses, an economics columnst at Turkish daily Radikal: "There was a sharp volatility in global markets before the protests. With growing protests we may see significant capital outflows and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may implement tightening policies. Inflation data may remain subdued if capital outflows accelerate." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 June 02, 2013 12:05 ET (16:05 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130602e9620004p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
