\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Some IMF directors say Turkey should raise rates\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anna Yukhananov   \par \uc2 468 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 November 2012\par 18:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details from IMF review, background on Turkey)\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, Nov 20 (Reuters) - Some directors on the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's executive board on Tuesday recommended that Turkey return to a positive real interest rate to target inflation.\par \par \uc2 The board's annual discussion of the Turkish economy came before \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  trimmed the highest of its main rates on Tuesday to prop up a slowing economy.\par \par \uc2 "Many directors saw merit in returning to a positive real policy rate under a conventional inflation-targeting framework, supported by a strengthened communication policy," the IMF said in a statement following the board discussion on Turkey, which happened on Nov. 16.\par \par \uc2 Many of the IMF's directors also recommended that \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  should use more sterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to manage risk from capital inflows, since it has a low level of international reserves. Central banks use sterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating without affecting the money supply.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's economy, the fastest growing in Europe last year, has slowed this year as domestic demand weakens. With recent rate cuts, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been trying to boost growth without allowing the lira to strengthen too sharply.\par \par \uc2 The prospect of a surge for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  grew last week when rating agency Fitch gave Turkey its first investment grade credit rating in 18 years, pushing the lira to a three-month high. While the ratings upgrade is a positive sign, it also increases the risk of cash from abroad flooding the country in the short term, further widening the current account deficit.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's current account deficit is its main economic weakness, reaching 10 percent of national output in 2011.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past has also had to act strongly to counter a jump in the lira's value - due in the past to ultra-low interest rates in the developed world - that would cripple both Turkey's exporters and economic growth.\par \par \uc2 But inflation still remains above target, the IMF board said. In its visit to the country earlier this year, the IMF said Turkey's annual inflation was around 9.2 percent in September, well above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's year-end target of 5 percent.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, the \uc2 Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it expects inflation to moderate to around 7 percent by the end of this year.\par \par \uc2 IMF directors welcomed Turkey's plans for a tighter fiscal policy, as proposed in its budget for 2013, and called on it to increase its primary surplus to boost national savings, which could reduce the economy's vulnerability to volatile capital flows.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Lucia Mutikani; Editing by James Dalgleish)\par \par \uc2 IMF-TURKEY/ (UPDATE 1) | C | D | E | M | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020121123e8bk000xq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
