\par\par \b \uc2 DJ After Scrambling to Lira Rescue, Turkey May Shift to Growth\b0\par\par\uc2 790 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juli 2012\par 18:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may loosen monetary stance to boost growth\par \par \uc2 -- Traders betting on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate cut drive down bond yields\par \par \uc2 -- Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  must be vigilant on lira to keep credibility\par \par \uc2 By Emre Peker\par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL--Barely six months after being forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore up the embattled lira, markets are betting \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may loosen monetary policy to aid the government's economic growth target. That could undermine the credibility Turkey's rate setters worked hard to build as investors questioned their unorthodox policies to combat inflation and restore financial stability.\par \par \uc2 Traders already expect Governor Erdem Basci to cut borrowing costs for banks and encourage credit growth. That pushed the benchmark two-year bond yield down 12 basis points to 8.16% on Thursday, the lowest since October. Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been varying interest rates daily between its benchmark rate of 5.75% and market rate of 11.5% since October to tame inflation and stabilize the lira. The funding rate has plummeted to about 8.5% in July from as high as 10.2% last month.\par \par \uc2 "The market sees more and more chances for the rate to come down," said Viktor Szabo, who helps manage $8 billion in emerging-market debt at \uc2 Aberdeen Asset Management\uc2  in London. "Even if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will ease, it will stay behind the curve. The market will price in more cuts than what the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will deliver; they would lose much credibility if they try to be ahead of the market and do a huge cut."\par \par \uc2 In recent months, Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been buoyed by an economic rebalancing in line with its expectations, driven by rising exports, declining domestic demand and slowing inflation. That, investors say, is increasing the likelihood Turkey will join its global peers by cutting its rate ceiling to boost growth.\par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut benchmark rates Thursday, citing no short-term inflationary risk as the euro zone faces a double-dip recession. Meanwhile, the Bank of England announced another round of stimulus and the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its benchmark rate for the second time in less than a month.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's gross domestic product expanded 3.2% in the first quarter, at about a quarter of the pace in the first three months of 2011, and consumer price inflation slowed to 8.87% in June from a three-and-a-half-year peak of 11.1% in April.\par \par \uc2 "If I was the bank, I'd wait a bit to see how things work out in the euro zone, but I suspect they might be keen to make an adjustment after those CPI numbers," said Caroline Gorman, who helps manage more than $7 billion in emerging-market assets at \uc2 GAM Ltd\uc2 . in London. "It's important that they maintain signals to the market that they are vigilant about lira stability because it plays an important psychological role."\par \par \uc2 To support Prime Minister Recep Tayyip Erdogan's 4% economic growth target for this year, Governor Basci may seek to release his hold on domestic credit growth and boost spending, investors and economists say.\par \par \uc2 Last year, credit-fueled domestic spending saw Turkey's ballooning current-account deficit hit a record $78.6 billion--more than 10% of gross domestic product--in October. Investors doubting Turkey's ability to finance the gap bet against the lira, driving it down 18% against the dollar in 2011. The lira's decline, the worst of any emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world, caused inflation to spike and prompted the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , spending precious reserves to buy liras and squeezing lenders to curb consumer spending.\par \par \uc2 "We expect domestic demand to recover from second quarter onwards," said Nilufer Sezgin, chief economist at Ekspres Invest, the Istanbul-based brokerage owned by \uc2 Dexia SA\uc2 's Denizbank.\par \par \uc2 Domestic private consumption didn't grow in real terms in the first three months of the year, BGC Partners Chief Economist Ozgur Altug wrote July 2, when the GDP figures for the first quarter were unveiled. Private-sector investment demand will be a main driver of economic growth in the second half of the year, said Sengul Dagdeviren, \uc2 ING Groep NV\uc2 's Istanbul-based economist.\par \par \uc2 "The overall tone with slower global growth and emerging-markets growth, and lower inflationary pressures all point to the possibility of keeping pretty loose monetary policies everywhere," Aberdeen's Mr. Szabo said in an interview. "And then, the search for yield starts, and Turkey stands out as one of the highest yields."\par \par \uc2 Write to Emre Peker at emre.peker@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 05, 2012 12:08 ET (16:08 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120705e875004i0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
