\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Turkish Lira Keeps Rising, But Markets Say Upside Is Limited\b0\par\par\uc2 By Yeliz Candemir and Joe Parkinson  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 479 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Januar 2012\par 16:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL (Dow Jones)--The Turkish lira strengthened for the fourth successive day Wednesday, underlining its position as one of the world's best performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year. However, economists say that although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strong \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks appears to have won the latest battle to shore up the lira, market concerns over the economy's imbalances mean the war is not yet over.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the lira was trading more than 0.1% higher against the dollar at 1.8380, capping a weekly gain of 1.6% and a strengthening of 3% since the beginning of the year. That makes the lira the best performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Europe, the Middle East and Africa in 2012, and only just behind the Indian rupee--the emerging market's top performer--with a 6% rise.\par \par \uc2 The winning streak marks quite a turnaround for the volatile lira, which in December touched 1.9205, a record low against the dollar as markets tested the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's resolve to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid deteriorating global sentiment and heightened nerves about Turkey's widening imbalances. But economists say the lira's march is likely to be halted by lingering market nerves over the economy's weakness and skepticism over Turkey's increasingly unorthodox monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 The recent gains come after Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in late December stepped up its fight to prevent a slide in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . On Dec. 30 the monetary policy committee launched a large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for only the second time in five years, following that move with four consecutive \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  of around $4.5 billion, according to traders. In further measures to prop up the lira, the bank has continued to hold its daily \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  auctions.\par \par \uc2 The bank also began a policy in October which allowed it to double the rate it charged banks to borrow to 12.5%, tightening liquidity and placing upward pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Tightening has become more imperative as Turkish inflation hit a three-year high of 10.45% in December.\par \par \uc2 Against this backdrop, Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor earlier this month boasted that the lira would be "one of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  gaining the most in 2012," and would "continue to appreciate this year because the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will let it appreciate."\par \par \uc2 Economists, however, remain cautious, pointing to a ballooning current account deficit forecast to have topped 10% of gross domestic product for the full-year of 2011. The cost of insuring Turkish debt has risen while the lira has firmed, indicating residual market nervousness.\par \par \uc2 -By Yeliz Candemir and Joe Parkinson, Dow Jones Newswires, +90 53 0613 0065;\par \par \uc2 joe.parkinson@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 18, 2012 10:46 ET (15:46 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120118e81i004io\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgo