\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ(1/12) Dismay Over Turkish Rates\b0\par\par\uc2 786 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2012\par 01:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par A10\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL) \par    By Joe Parkinson \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ISTANBUL -- Economists expressed dismay Wednesday at a pledge from Prime Minister Recep Tayyip Erdogan to strike back at an "interest-rate lobby" that allegedly seeks to stifle Turkish growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 In comments late Tuesday, Mr. Erdogan also said market interest rates should be lower. High interest rates, he said, cause inflation -- contrary to orthodox economic theory accepted around the globe. "We will make the necessary sharp responses against the interest-rate lobby. We won't let the interest-rate lobby work in comfort," he told reporters in Ankara.\par \par \uc2 The Turkish leader hasn't outlined who he believes constitutes this lobby. But he is commonly understood to be referring to foreign parties -- including investors, economists and journalists -- who have called for an increase in Turkey's benchmark policy rate in order to tame inflation and cool an overheating economy. Government ministers have suggested the call for higher rates comes from those who want to park their money in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  and "suck \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's blood."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Erdogan's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes as economists covering \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  say they are struggling to understand the country's monetary policy, which they fear could drive its economy, which grew 8.2% in the third quarter of 2011, toward a hard landing and even recession later this year.\par \par \uc2 The stakes are high. The government's electoral success and increased international clout are based largely on growing prosperity, successes that a sudden downturn could damage. Turkey, which conducts roughly half of its trade with the European Union, has also been a rare bright spot among European economies.\par \par \uc2 "Markets find these comments a little disturbing. . . . There's absolutely no evidence of a speculative attack on the lira, or some foreign-markets plot against Turkey," said Tim Ash, chief emerging-markets economist at RBS in London. "Worse, it comes as the market is increasingly confused by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy strategy; it's either going to win them a Nobel Prize or it's a road to nowhere."\par \par \uc2 At issue, as the inflation rate has risen above 10% for the first time in three years, is the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reluctance to raise its main policy rate. That benchmark overnight rate controls lending for all institutions, and now stands at 5.75%. Instead, the bank has employed a wider interest-rate corridor -- a rate that it says will vary between 5.75% and 12.5% -- that it can change daily, enabling it to tighten and loosen policy according to market conditions.\par \par \uc2 Turkish ministers have called for the policy rate to be equal to the rate of inflation -- in effect, for a 0% real interest rate. In May, Mr. Erdogan said Turkey should have real interest rates at zero, prompting some economists to speculate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been keeping its policy rate artificially low to accommodate that goal.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't respond to calls to comment on Mr. Erdogan's speech.\par \par \uc2 Confusion over the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's intentions have contributed to a rapid fall in the Turkish lira's value. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has attempted to stem that fall by spending scarce foreign reserves in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -exchange market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The bank says it spent some $4.5 billion in the last week of December, bringing its reserves to $78.3 billion.\par \par \uc2 "However cruelly the interest-rate lobby works, the spending power of citizens of this country who consume declines by the same amount," Mr. Erdogan said. Market interest rates should fall to the 5.75% policy rate, he said.\par \par \uc2 Lenders such as \uc2 Garanti Bankasi\uc2  AS are now charging Turkish borrowers as much as 20% annually for loans, up from a little over 10% a year ago, crimping consumer borrowing.\par \par \uc2 In a year when the government hopes to adopt a new constitution, likely in a referendum, any shadow over Turkey's economy could pose a direct political threat to the government. Analysts say that could explain Mr. Erdogan's finger-pointing at an alleged interest-rate lobby.\par \par \uc2 A representative for the prime minister declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Markets shrugged off Mr. Erdogan's comments, with Turkish assets rising on news that the economy's bloated current-account deficit in November narrowed for the first time in more than two years.\par \par \uc2 What Mr. Erdogan says on interest rates shouldn't matter, as Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legally independent, with a mandate to fight inflation and ensure financial stability. But speculation over the bank's independence has risen since April, when Erdem Basci, a childhood friend of Turkish economic czar and deputy prime minister Ali Babacan, was named bank governor.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 11, 2012 19:43 ET (00:43 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120112e81c0008s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
