\par\par \b \uc2 MIDEAST MONEY-Turkey to stay course on monetary policy--but for how long?\b0\par\par\uc2 1,072 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Januar 2012\par 15:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Unorthodox policy has so far supported fast growth\par \par \uc2 * C.bank suggests ultra-low rates needed given global uncertainty\par \par \uc2 * But inflation is not responding, weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  a concern\par \par \uc2 * Many analysts would prefer simpler, traditional policy mix\par \par \uc2 * Other countries with unorthodox policies ran into trouble\par \par \uc2 By Seltem Iyigun and Seda Sezer\par \par \uc2 ANKARA, Jan 4 (Reuters) - Turkey bade farewell to the old year promising to stay the course with its unorthodox monetary policy, but some analysts expect that before long, it may have to rethink its stance because of economic threats lurking in 2012.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy, which consists of keeping interest rates low, raising required reserve ratios for commercial banks and widening the gap between its borrowing and lending rates, aims to counter a huge current account deficit while balancing the risks associated with the volatile lira \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The policy has helped keep Turkish growth robust over the past year while other economies, particularly in Europe, have wilted. But it has left many investors confused over the complex mix, and many are worried about the risk of a "hard landing" -- a sudden, destructive slowdown of growth -- in 2012.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has been criticised by foreign fund managers for what it calls its "flexibility", has been reluctant to raise interest rates because this would hamper growth, even though inflation was above 10 percent at the end of 2011, its highest level in three years.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the bank has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by selling dollars to prop up the weak lira, which lost more than 20 percent of its value against the U.S. dollar last year.\par \par \uc2 "Turkey will have to make a choice on whether its priority will be growth or inflation," said Imran Ahmad, emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at RBS.\par \par \uc2 "In the end policy makers can't have it all. Turkey can't have it both ways. There has to be a trade-off. There is going to be more pressure this year on policy makers to respond."\par \par \uc2 PERFORMANCE\par \par \uc2 Turkey, which until a decade ago was viewed by international investors as a financial basket-case, was one of the world's best-peforming economies in 2011, propelled by roaring domestic demand from its growing middle class. While Europe has struggled with anaemic growth and a sovereign debt crisis, Ankara estimates growth was 8 percent last year.\par \par \uc2 But the economy is widely expected to slow considerably in 2012 -- the International Monetary Fund predicts growth will fall to 2 percent. The Turkish government's medium-term economic plan anticipates growth of 4 percent in 2012.\par \par \uc2 By reducing the expansion of domestic demand, an economic slowdown could help to solve Turkey's problems. It could help to bring down the current account deficit, which at around 10 percent of gross domestic product is one of the biggest threats to economic stability. It might also cut inflation, which the government wants to limit to 5 percent in 2012.\par \par \uc2 Policy makers insist Turkey's slowdown and the crisis in the European Union, to which Turkey sends over 40 percent of its exports, will vindicate their approach. But analysts fear the numbers may not add up.\par \par \uc2 "Even with a reasonable growth rate of 2-3 percent, the current account-to-GDP ratio would only decrease 2-2.5 percent," said Haluk Burumcekci, chief economist at EFG Istanbul.\par \par \uc2 Many economists would therefore prefer a simpler, more traditional monetary policy mix: higher interest rates to strengthen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which would help to bring down the current account deficit and inflation, combined with lower reserve ratios if necessary to encourage banks to lend.\par \par \uc2 Other emerging economies tried unorthodox monetary policies last year and got themselves into trouble. Kenya, once a darling of emerging market investors, cut interest rates early in 2011 to spur growth; when inflation surged, it responded by adjusting money market liqidity rather than raising rates. The result was a confidence crisis in Kenya's markets that dragged its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to record lows.\par \par \uc2 Turkey has a much stronger and more diversified economy than Kenya, but as the lira's volatility suggests, it risks its own loss of investor confidence.\par \par \uc2 STAY THE COURSE\par \par \uc2 Announcing monetary and exchange rate policy for 2012 at a news conference on Dec. 27, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Erdem Basci said the bank would stick to its current policy in a climate of global uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 That means the bank will continue to use reserve ratios -- the proportion of their funds which commercial banks must hold back from lending -- and the gap between its borrowing and lending rates as major policy tools, although its main policy interest rate could be adjusted. That rate, the one-week repo, is at a record-low 5.75 percent.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tries to be clear about its policies but the framework of its new monetary policy is so complex that the bank can't avoid the uncertainty," said Nilufer Sezgin, chief economist at Ekspres Invest.\par \par \uc2 Tufan Comert, a strategist from Garanti Securities, said the bank's policies were having a limited impact on inflation because Turkey's inflation had deep structural roots. The country has a large energy import bill, and among emerging economies, it is believed to be one of the most susceptible to having \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness pass through into inflation.\par \par \uc2 "The Bank is going to press ahead with the flexible policy," with even more flexibility," Comert said. "This means markets will carry on trying to see the way ahead. Medium or long-term planning will not be possible for some time. This is not what markets want."\par \par \uc2 The results, analysts said, is likely to be inflation remaining uncomfortably high. The World Bank's Turkey representative Ulrich Zachau has said Turkey's 5 percent inflation target for 2012 will be hard to reach because of the pass-through effect from the weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Burumcekci of EFG Istanbul expects inflation to start decreasing significantly only after October. And the lira, despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , looks set to stay under pressure.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been using \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but that policy might not be sustainable," said Ahmad at RBS. "In the context of a weak global growth and deleveraging, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have to use monetary policy to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 (Writing by Ibon Villelabeitia; Editing by Andrew Torchia)\par \par \uc2 TURKEY-ECONOMY/ANALYSIS   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120107e814000b3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\