\par\par \b \uc2 Turkey's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Attempts To Fight Financial Markets\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joe Parkinson   \par \uc2 544 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Oktober 2011\par 16:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL (Dow Jones)--Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped back into the ring this week in the latest round of what's turning into quite a dust-up.\par \par \uc2 In the red corner: heavyweight financial markets that have persisted in selling the lira despite repeated signals from \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy makers that they will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to put a floor under the embattled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the blue corner: Ankara's plucky central bankers -- a determined bunch not afraid to throw some big punches, as on Tuesday when the bank directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market for the first time in five years after a record \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  auction failed to lift the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The battle to bully the historically volatile lira, which has fallen close to 20% against the dollar this year, is nothing new. But it has intensified since August as the market focused on fast-growing Turkey's structural weakness: a soaring current account deficit forecast to top 9% of GDP this year. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the summer has repeatedly offered bulky dollar auctions to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was under strong selling pressure as a toxic combination of global economic fears and escalating euro-zone debt worries led investors to fold their bets in emerging markets on a colossal scale.\par \par \uc2 But there's a problem. Those \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  don't seem to be working, and markets are increasingly nervous the bank has started a fight it doesn't have the firepower to finish.\par \par \uc2 That's because Turkey is running one of the lowest levels of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves relative to short-term debt exposure of any emerging market, giving it limited resources to defend the lira. That, in turn, is boosting market expectations that policy makers will have to begin raising rates aggressively to offset the depletion of reserves.\par \par \uc2 "The Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is laying out its stall, showing a willingness to take on the market... [it] is clearly aimed at sending a message to the market that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have no truck with speculation, and speculators, against the lira," said Timothy Ash, an emerging markets strategist at \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland Group PLC\uc2  in London.\par \par \uc2 "Markets will definitely continue to test them, and the bank will need to stay on the market for some time," said Simon Quijano-Evans, chief economist for Europe, the Middle East and Africa at ING in London. "That begs the question: How long can they continue?"\par \par \uc2 Certainly, Turkey's approximately $86 billion reserves set against its ballooning current account deficit appear to leave the economy more exposed than many other emerging market economies.\par \par \uc2 South Africa, which also faces a marked depreciation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , holds reserves of $43 billion but only runs a current account deficit of 3% of GDP. Resource-rich Brazil has reserves of $351 billion and runs a 2% deficit, while Russia, which also recently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to manage the ruble, holds a whopping $510 billion and runs a current account surplus of $100 billion.\par \par \uc2 Markets will be watching closely for clues whether Turkey's top bankers will try to stare down the traders or move to raise rates, and risk slowing growth, to shore up the lira. [ 10-21-11 1049ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020111021e7al000c0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
