\par\par \b \uc2 DJ UPDATE: Turkey  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Holds Key Interest Rate At 5.75%\b0\par\par\uc2 544 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Oktober 2011\par 15:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Rewrites, adding market reaction, economist comment, further detail throughout.)\par \par \uc2 By Joe Parkinson\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL (Dow Jones)--Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday held its key interest rate at a record low as expected, but increased the overnight lending rate and issued a hawkish statement in an effort to shore up the embattled lira.\par \par \uc2 Policy makers kept the one-week repurchase agreement interest rate unchanged for the second straight month at 5.75%, according to a statement on the bank's website.\par \par \uc2 That decision was in line with the expectations of seven economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires, who unanimously forecast no change.\par \par \uc2 The bank also held the overnight borrowing rate steady at 5%, but in a surprise move, raised the overnight lending rate to 12.5% from 9%.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  summarized its decisions in a statement Thursday that was markedly more hawkish than those made previously, dropping all mention of any potential policy loosening and stressing that the lira's recent depreciation would spur a significant rise in inflation in the coming period.\par \par \uc2 The lira initially jumped on the news, but gains were short-lived, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moving back up to where it was trading before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's announcement. At 1114 GMT, the dollar was trading at TRY1.8617 against the Turkish lira, after falling to as low as TRY1.8564 immediately after decision.\par \par \uc2 Economists said the bank's more hawkish tone showed policy makers were regearing away from a loosening bias to combat intensifying pressure on the lira.\par \par \uc2 "The tone is clearly much more hawkish and this is the first step towards a key interest rate hike... the removal of the talk about rate cuts and general tone on the inflation outlook is clearly more hawkish," said Simon Quijano-Evans, chief EMEA economist with ING in London.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly to support the lira for the first time in five years, amid persistent pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  which has seen it fall some 20% against the dollar since November.\par \par \uc2 That move was intended to demonstrate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's determination to halt the lira's depreciation by buying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than raising interest rates which would risk curbing Turkey's economic growth, economists said.\par \par \uc2 Although Thursday's statement has bolstered the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's position, some economists still question whether policy makers are doing enough.\par \par \uc2 Turkey has among the lowest levels of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves relative to short-term debt exposure of any emerging market, giving it limited resources to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . As a result, there is much skepticism among market participants over the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to continue supporting the lira.\par \par \uc2 "The question in terms of today's move by the CBRT is whether it is hawkish enough... the underlying concern is that the CBRT is still too dovish to raise the interest rates that really matter... this is more a signalling story than serious effort to stop a speculative attack," said Tim Ash an economist at RBS.\par \par \uc2 -By Joe Parkinson and Yeliz Candemir, Dow Jones Newswires; +905306130065, joe.parkinson@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 20, 2011 09:19 ET (13:19 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020111020e7ak00465\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
