\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ.COM What's News - Worldwide News Briefs For Oct 19\b0\par\par\uc2 893 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2011\par 22:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN WEIGHS REVISING PLAN ON CARBON CUTS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan is considering revising plans to cut carbon dioxide emissions by 25% by 2020 as the March nuclear disaster complicates the country's energy future.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par KURDISH ATTACK KILLS 24 IN TURKEY \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kurdish militants killed at least 24 Turkish troops on Turkey's border with Iraq, in one of the deadliest attacks since the Kurdish Workers Party began its armed campaign almost thirty years ago.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CLINTON IN KABUL FOR AFGHAN TALKS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. Secretary of State Hillary Rodham Clinton is in Afghanistan on an unannounced visit aimed at encouraging the country's wary leadership to keep up Taliban reconciliation efforts as the Obama administration presses ahead with troop-withdrawal plans.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par KENYA SAYS SOMALI MILITANT ATTACKS HURTING ECONOMY \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Kenyan government sent troops into Somalia to search for people kidnapped by Somali gunmen and to push Islamist militants away from Kenya's tourist destinations, the country's tourism minister said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STRONG FRANC HURTS SWISS BUSINESS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A growing number of Swiss businesses have shut down production facilities and laid off staff over the past few weeks as the continued strength of the franc takes its toll, prompting fresh calls for another decisive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par THAKSIN ALLY IS NAMED THAI POLICE CHIEF \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Priewpan Damapong, a political ally of former Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra, was appointed Thailand's new national police chief.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DEMAND AT HOME AIDS CHINA GROWTH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although China's economic growth slowed in the third quarter, the data suggest a nation that has long relied on exports is getting more help from domestic demand-a potential positive for the global economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPANESE SEEK OUT 'HOT SPOTS' \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prompted by recent discoveries of radioactive material far from the troubled Fukushima plant, the government will instruct citizens on how to detect and decontaminate 'hot spots.'\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FLOODS SET BACK NEW THAI LEADER \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Recriminations over the handling of Thailand's worst floods in half a century are causing fresh setbacks for Prime Minister Yingluck Shinawatra's fledgling government.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PRISONER SWAP RESETS MIDEAST EQUATION \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Palestinian militant group Hamas freed an Israeli soldier, Gilad Shalit, held captive for five years in exchange for hundreds of prisoners, in an unprecedented deal with the potential to begin a new phase in relations between sworn enemies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CLINTON MAKES AID TRIP TO LIBYA \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Secretary of State Hillary Rodham Clinton made an unannounced visit to Tripoli and pledged millions of dollars in new aid, including assistance to secure weaponry that many fear could fall into the hands of terrorists.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BRUSSELS COOLS TIES WITH UKRAINE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European Union said a meeting with Ukrainian President Viktor Yanukovych had been postponed after he rejected calls from Western capitals to release the jailed former Prime Minister Yulia Tymoshenko.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EU MAY REVISIT CLIMATE STANCE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European Union is for the first time questioning whether it should press ahead with plans to cut greenhouse gas emissions if other countries don't follow.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMAN HIGH-SPEED TRADERS OFF HOOK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Germany's main exchange operator found that financial institutions, not high-frequency traders, caused a miniature "flash crash" in August.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 TURKEY INTERVENES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TO BOOST LIRA \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The move is aimed to demonstrate to markets the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's determination to halt the lira's fall by buying up \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , rather than raise interest rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CAR BOMB IN SOMALI CAPITAL KILLS 4 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A suicide car bomb exploded near Somalia's Foreign Ministry, killing at least four people, including the bomber, as government leaders met in the capital with Kenya's ministers of defense and foreign affairs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par IMMOLATIONS PRESSURE TIBET EXILE LEADER \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Amid a wave of self-immolations by Tibetans, the new leader of Tibet's government-in-exile discouraged such gestures but called for support for nuns and monks protesting China's policies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BERLUSCONI'S LEGAL BURDEN LIGHTENS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A Milan court refused to indict Italian Premier Silvio Berlusconi in a tax fraud case involving his \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mediaset\f28 \uc2 \f2  media company.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NEW ZEALAND SHIP SPILLS MORE OIL \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A stranded cargo vessel off New Zealand has spilled more oil into the ocean, as efforts to pump the remaining oil off the ship are hampered by bad weather.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDONESIAN MINISTERS NAMED \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesian President Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono named the remaining members of his new cabinet on Tuesday; his choices are unlikely to rock the boat, analysts said, and may not be able to push through needed economic changes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FILIPINO SOLDIERS CLASH WITH REBELS; 18 KILLED \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Philippine troops battled Muslim guerrillas in a volatile southern province Tuesday in fighting that killed at least 18 combatants and left 10 soldiers missing, officials said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SINGH SEES INDIA, BRAZIL, SOUTH AFRICA COORDINATION AT G-20 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil, India and South Africa should negotiate as a block at the Group of 20 meeting of leading nations in Paris next month, Indian Prime Minister Manmohan Singh said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UGANDAN POLICE TO CHARGE PROTESTORS WITH TREASON \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ugandan police accused opponents of President Yoweri Museveni of a plot to overthrow the government and said 15 people arrested in antigovernment protests this week would be charged with treason.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MADAGASCAR PREMIER RESIGNS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Madagascar's coup-appointed premier has resigned along with his government to open the way for a consensus government to end the Indian Ocean island's two-year political crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (For continuously updated news from the Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com}}}\uc2 \f2 .) [ 10-19-11 1645ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020111019e7aj000ve\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
