\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ.COM What's News - Worldwide News Briefs For Oct 19\b0\par\par\uc2 423 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2011\par 18:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par KURDISH ATTACK KILLS 24 IN TURKEY \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kurdish militants killed at least 24 Turkish troops on Turkey's border with Iraq, in one of the deadliest attacks since the Kurdish Workers Party began its armed campaign almost thirty years ago.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par KENYA SAYS SOMALI MILITANT ATTACKS HURTING ECONOMY \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Kenyan government sent troops into Somalia to search for people kidnapped by Somali gunmen and to push Islamist militants away from Kenya's tourist destinations, the country's tourism minister said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DEMAND AT HOME AIDS CHINA GROWTH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although China's economic growth slowed in the third quarter, the data suggest a nation that has long relied on exports is getting more help from domestic demand-a potential positive for the global economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPANESE SEEK OUT 'HOT SPOTS' \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prompted by recent discoveries of radioactive material far from the troubled Fukushima plant, the government will instruct citizens on how to detect and decontaminate 'hot spots.'\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FLOODS SET BACK NEW THAI LEADER \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Recriminations over the handling of Thailand's worst floods in half a century are causing fresh setbacks for Prime Minister Yingluck Shinawatra's fledgling government.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PRISONER SWAP RESETS MIDEAST EQUATION \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Palestinian militant group Hamas freed an Israeli soldier, Gilad Shalit, held captive for five years in exchange for hundreds of prisoners, in an unprecedented deal with the potential to begin a new phase in relations between sworn enemies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CLINTON MAKES AID TRIP TO LIBYA \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Secretary of State Hillary Rodham Clinton made an unannounced visit to Tripoli and pledged millions of dollars in new aid, including assistance to secure weaponry that many fear could fall into the hands of terrorists.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BRUSSELS COOLS TIES WITH UKRAINE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European Union said a meeting with Ukrainian President Viktor Yanukovych had been postponed after he rejected calls from Western capitals to release the jailed former Prime Minister Yulia Tymoshenko.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EU MAY REVISIT CLIMATE STANCE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European Union is for the first time questioning whether it should press ahead with plans to cut greenhouse gas emissions if other countries don't follow.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMAN HIGH-SPEED TRADERS OFF HOOK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Germany's main exchange operator found that financial institutions, not high-frequency traders, caused a miniature "flash crash" in August.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 TURKEY INTERVENES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TO BOOST LIRA \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The move is aimed to demonstrate to markets the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's determination to halt the lira's fall by buying up \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , rather than raise interest rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (For continuously updated news from the Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com }}}\uc2 \f2 .) [ 10-19-11 1221ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020111019e7aj000fm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
