\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ:  \highlight22\uc2 Turkey Central Bank Intervenes \highlight\uc2  To Boost Lira; First Time Since 2006\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joe Parkinson and Marc Champion  \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL  \par \uc2 767 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 2011\par 19:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly to support the lira for the first time in five years Tuesday, taking on skeptical \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets after an earlier U.S. dollar auction failed to lift the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The move aimed to demonstrate to markets the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's determination to halt the lira's fall by buying up \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than raise interest rates, which would risk damping Turkey's rapid economic growth, economists said.\par \par \uc2 Turkey, however, has among the lowest levels of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves relative to short term debt exposure of any emerging market, giving it limited resources to defend the lira. As a result the bank is fighting growing skepticism over its ability to see the policy through.\par \par \uc2 "Markets will definitely continue to test them and the bank will need to stay on the market for some time. That begs the question: how long can they continue?" said Simon Quijano-Evans, chief economist for Europe, the Middle East and Africa at ING in London.\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 Traders reported that the bank began buying liras around 4 p.m. local time, sending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  almost 1% higher to TRY1.85 to the dollar, after an earlier sale of $750 million at auction failed to have any significant impact. By evening the lira had lost part of that gain, trading just above TRY1.86 to the dollar, though it was still up on the day.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  later confirmed in an emailed statement that it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market due to "speculative activity" and "unhealthy price moves." The statement also said the bank might hold another large dollar auction Wednesday, after Tuesday's record offering of $1.35 billion.\par \par \uc2 The statement didn't name the size of the Tuesday's direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however, and a bank spokesman also declined to give the size. The last time the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Republic of \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets was June 26, 2006, when it spent $848 million.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's lira, like several other emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has come under intense selling pressure in recent weeks as a toxic combination of global economy fears and escalating euro-zone debt worries led investors to fold their bets in emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't the only one to have sold reserves to stem the tide. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia this month bought 10 billion rubles after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tumbled. South Africa's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , by contrast, appears to have taken the view that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is futile, economists said.\par \par \uc2 While a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is good for exports, it also makes imports of energy, raw materials and consumer goods more expensive, stoking inflation. Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  currently forecasts inflation to hit 7.5% at the end of the year, above its 5.5% target.\par \par \uc2 What makes Turkey vulnerable, despite an otherwise healthy economy, is the size of its current account deficit, now above 9% of gross domestic product, economists say. The International Monetary Fund has advised the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise interest rates and stop selling dollars--the opposite of what it is doing--so that it can build up reserves against any sudden withdrawal of external financing for the roughly $70 billion deficit.\par \par \uc2 The scale of the current account deficit reflects Turkey's rapid recent growth--10.2% in the first half of 2011--and traditionally low savings rate, a structural problem the government says it is aware of and preparing to address.\par \par \uc2 The bank's lira policy will fail unless good news in Europe or the U.S. reverses the current flow of money from emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Atilla Yesilada, an Istanbul-based economist with Global Source Partners. "There is no speculative attack on the Turkish lira in particular. This is like trying to resist a tsunami using sandbags," he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Yesilada estimates that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has at most $10 billion in its war chest that it can use to defend the lira. "If they run through that, then foreign investors would become concerned by the very level of the Turkish reserves and start selling the lira on that," making the problem worse rather than better, he said.\par \par \uc2 The lira has weakened 30% against the dollar since November 2010.\par \par \uc2 -By Joe Parkinson and Marc Champion, The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 -Yeliz Candemir contributed to this article. [ 10-18-11 1331ET ]\par \par \uc2 D