\par\par \b \uc2 DJ UPDATE: Turkey August Industrial Output Eases More Than Expected\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joe Parkinson and Yeliz Candemir  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 429 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 2011\par 11:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL (Dow Jones)--Turkish industrial production--a leading indicator of economic output--slowed sharply in August, significantly undershooting market expectations and suggesting that momentum is easing in one of the world's fastest-growing economies.\par \par \uc2 According to data published Monday by the national statistics institute TUIK, industrial output rose 3.8% on the year in August, almost halving the 6.9% expansion record in July. The annual easing, which substantially undershot the 5.0% growth expected by the market, was driven by a 7.1% contraction on the month amid broad-based declines across the production subsectors.\par \par \uc2 The news had little effect on Turkish assets, initially trimming the lira's early gains against the dollar but failing to erase Turkish stocks' morning advance.\par \par \uc2 Despite the pronounced easing, economists said the data indicated that Turkey's economy was increasingly likely to navigate a soft landing from a rapid growth rate that many had feared was overheating the economy.\par \par \uc2 "The soft landing continues," said Ozgur Altug, chief economist at BCG Partners in a research note, adding that "while the data look worse than expected, it is clear that the economy is normalizing after a sharp growth in the first half of 2011."\par \par \uc2 Turkey outstripped China to post the fastest growth of any group-of-twenty economy in the first half of 2011, confirming its position as Eurasia's rising tiger as rampant consumer demand propelled an expansion of 10.2% versus the first six months of 2010. The pace of domestic demand, underpinned by a rapid expansion of consumer lending, had in recent months spurred fears in the market that Turkey's economy was overheating and could face a painful correction.\par \par \uc2 But recent indicators have pointed to a pronounced slowdown, underscoring the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's prognosis that output would ease significantly in the second half of the year.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  strongly in the market last week to stop an abrupt slide in the lira, worries that a global downturn--particularly in the European Union, Turkey's dominant trade partner--could take Turkey's booming economy down with it.\par \par \uc2 As the global economic outlook has darkened, Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has turned to foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  auctions to stabilize the lira, rather than raising interest rates, which could damp growth.\par \par \uc2 -By Joe Parkinson and Yeliz Candemir, Dow Jones Newswires, +905354320591; joe.parkinson@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 10, 2011 05:04 ET (09:04 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020111010e7aa0027o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
